---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Eclipses', href: '/explore/eclipses/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Eclipses in Astrology, what they mark and how often"
  description="Solar and lunar eclipses occur in pairs a few times a year near the lunar nodes. What astrologers read into them, why they run in 18-month cycles, and how to hold them without dread."
  path="/explore/eclipses/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Eclipses</h1>
        <p class="lede">Eclipses are new and full moons with the volume turned up. They arrive in seasons, follow an 18-year rhythm, and mark chapters rather than verdicts.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.src}
        srcset={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.alt}
        width={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.width}
        height={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What makes a moon an eclipse</h2>
      <p>
        A <a href="/explore/glossary/#new-moon">new moon</a> happens every month when the Moon
        passes between Earth and Sun. Most months it passes slightly above or below, because the
        Moon's orbit is tilted about five degrees from the plane of Earth's orbit. But twice a year,
        the alignment lands near one of the two points where those planes intersect, the
        <a href="/explore/lunar-nodes/">lunar nodes</a>, and the shadows line up. A new moon there
        becomes a solar eclipse; a full moon there becomes a lunar eclipse.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because the geometry requires that alignment, eclipses cluster: they come in
        <strong>eclipse seasons</strong> of roughly a month, about twice a year, usually two or
        three eclipses at a time. In 2026, for instance, the seasons fell in February–March and
        August, each pairing a solar and a lunar eclipse two weeks apart.
      </p>

      <h2>The cycles worth knowing</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Roughly every 6 months</strong>: a new eclipse season arrives.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Roughly every 18–19 months</strong>: the nodes change sign, so eclipses move to a
          new pair of opposite signs and a new pair of houses in your chart.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Roughly every 18–19 years</strong>: the whole pattern repeats in the same signs
          (the Saros rhythm). Astrologers often look back to what was happening 19 years ago for the
          thematic rhyme.
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.distanceUnderEclipse} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What astrologers read into them</h2>
      <p>
        The traditional reading is acceleration. Eclipses tend to correspond with chapters that turn
        faster than usual: things conclude, arrive, or become undeniable in the houses the eclipse
        touches. Solar eclipses, being new moons, lean toward beginnings and openings; lunar
        eclipses, being full moons, lean toward culminations and releases.
      </p>
      <p>
        The honest caveat: eclipse effects are frequently overstated. Not every eclipse coincides
        with a life event, plenty of them pass without incident, and their reputation for calamity
        is a modern media inheritance rather than a technical claim. What is reliably true is that
        the axis being eclipsed, say the Leo–Aquarius axis while the nodes sit there, describes
        which pair of life areas is under revision for the eighteen months or so that the nodes
        remain.
      </p>

      <h2>How to hold an eclipse season</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Don't force decisions.</strong> The common counsel is to let eclipse periods
          reveal rather than to act aggressively into them. Information tends to still be arriving.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Watch the houses, not the headlines.</strong> An eclipse in your career house and
          an eclipse in your home house describe entirely different seasons, in the same sky.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Give it a fortnight.</strong> Eclipse pairs work as a unit; what opens at the
          solar eclipse often resolves near the lunar one two weeks later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Skip the dread.</strong> Eclipses are ordinary astronomy on a predictable
          schedule. They mark change, which is the one constant your chart was always going to
          contain.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        See <a href="/current-sky/">the current eclipse dates</a>, or read about
        <a href="/explore/lunar-nodes/">the lunar nodes</a> that make them possible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Which houses do your eclipses fall in?"
    body="That's a chart question, not a calendar question, and it changes what an eclipse season means for you."
    buttonLabel="See the readings"
    buttonHref="/readings/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
